            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Jesus Christ (the Lion of the Tribe of Judah), representing messianic kingship, divine power, victory, and authority.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Revelation 5:5\",\"Genesis 49:9\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Satan or demonic opposition, representing a predatory, destructive, and devouring threat.\",\"basis\":[\"1 Peter 5:8\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Hostile nations, wicked empires (such as Babylon), or oppressive human enemies.\",\"basis\":[\"Daniel 7:4\",\"Psalm 22:13\",\"2 Timothy 4:17\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The terrifying prophetic voice, judgment, or wrath of God against disobedience.\",\"basis\":[\"Amos 3:8\",\"Hosea 5:14\",\"Hosea 13:7-8\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"The boldness, courage, and fearlessness of the righteous.\",\"basis\":[\"Proverbs 28:1\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The lion is widely recognized in biblical exegesis as a prime example of polyvalent symbolism. Its meaning is entirely context-dependent, famously serving as a metaphor for both the conquering Christ and the predatory Devil, as well as earthly empires and divine judgment.\"}",
